---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getAccessoriesByCategory } from '../../lib/db';

const categoryData = getAccessoriesByCategory('hygrometers');
const accessories = categoryData?.accessories ?? [];
const sorted = [...accessories].sort((a, b) => {
  if (a.accScore != null && b.accScore != null) return b.accScore - a.accScore;
  if (a.accScore != null) return -1;
  if (b.accScore != null) return 1;
  return `${a.brand} ${a.model}`.localeCompare(`${b.brand} ${b.model}`);
});
const anyScored = sorted.some((a) => a.accScore != null);
---

<Layout
  title="Best Hygrometers (and How to Calibrate One) | Cigar Codex Guides"
  description="Analog vs digital hygrometers, why calibration matters more than the price tag, and how to salt-test one yourself."
>
  <div class="home-hero">
    <div class="cigar-hero__eyebrow">Buying Guide</div>
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">Best Hygrometers (and How to Calibrate One)</h1>
  </div>

  <section class="section summary-review">
    <p>
      A hygrometer is only as good as its calibration — an expensive digital unit reading 8% off
      is worse than a cheap one you've verified yourself. Calibrating takes fifteen minutes and no
      special equipment, and it matters more than which hygrometer you buy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Analog vs digital</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Analog (dial) hygrometers</strong> use a mechanical coil that expands and contracts
      with humidity. They're inexpensive and battery-free, but the mechanism drifts over time and
      needs more frequent rechecking than a digital sensor.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Digital hygrometers</strong> use an electronic sensor, usually more accurate out of
      the box, and some offer a genuine recalibration function rather than just a cosmetic
      "zero" screw. A few add Bluetooth monitoring so you can check a humidor from your phone — a
      convenience feature, not an accuracy one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">The salt test: calibrating any hygrometer yourself</h2>
    <p>
      This is the standard method and works on any hygrometer, analog or digital:
    </p>
    <p>
      1. Fill a bottle cap with ordinary table salt and add a few drops of water — just enough to
      dampen it, not dissolve it into a liquid.
    </p>
    <p>
      2. Place the cap and your hygrometer together in a small sealed container (a sandwich bag
      works) and leave them for 8-12 hours at room temperature.
    </p>
    <p>
      3. A damp-salt environment always settles at 75% relative humidity. Whatever your hygrometer
      reads at the end of the test is its offset — if it reads 79%, it's running 4 points high, and
      you should subtract 4 from all future readings (or use the unit's calibration screw/button to
      correct it directly, if it has one).
    </p>
    <p>
      Do this when a hygrometer is new, and recheck every six months or so — more often for analog
      dials, which drift faster than digital sensors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">What we've found so far</h2>
    {!anyScored && (
      <p class="placeholder-note">
        None of the hygrometers we've researched yet have three independent review sources. Below
        is what we found on each, including which ones offer real user-adjustable calibration
        versus a cosmetic zero screw — a more useful distinction than the star rating for this
        category.
      </p>
    )}
    {sorted.length > 0 ? (
      <div class="search-results">
        {sorted.map((a) => (
          <a class="cigar-card" href={`/accessories/hygrometers/${a.slug}/`}>
            <div class="cigar-card__brand">{a.brand}</div>
            <div class="cigar-card__name">{a.model}</div>
            <div class="cigar-card__score">
              Codex Score {a.accScore != null ? Math.round(a.accScore) : 'insufficient data'}
            </div>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    ) : (
      <p class="placeholder-note">Nothing recorded in this category yet.</p>
    )}
  </section>
</Layout>
